all_comments_text:
primetomas: Looks like a formatting issue in the passed in ipv6 issue? This is the pattern used to judge if it is an ipv6 address. private static final String ipv6Pattern = "^([\\dA-F]{1,4}:|((?=.*(::))(?!.*\\3.+\\3))\\3?)([\\dA-F]{1,4}(\\3|:\\b)|\\2){5}(([\\dA-F]{1,4}(\\3|:\\b|$)|\\2){2}|(((2[0-4]|1\\d|[1-9])?\\d|25[0-5])\\.?\\b){4})\\z"; Matcher m2 = StringTools.VALID_IPV6_PATTERN.matcher(ipAddress); return m2.matches();
hfzqasim: Alright, I think this pattern is not fully correct as it expects HEX characters A-F in capitals, but IPv6 address can be given in small HEX characters also. Moreover, I am trying different IPv6 address formats and still I am not able to get a certificate having IPv6 address as its SAN Examples I have tried FD00:DEED:ACDC:1908:0:0:0:208 FD00:DEED:ACDC:1908::208 FD00:DEED:ACDC:1908:0000:0000:0000:0208 All these examples are not working, could you provide an IPv6 address example which should work?
primetomas: Here are some examples we have in the test for this method: final String ipV6Example1 = "2001:0db8:3333:4444:5555:6666:7777:8888"; final String ipV6Example3 = "2001:db8::"; final String ipV6Example4 = "2001:db8::1234:5678"; final String ipV6Example5 = "::1234:5678"; final String ipV6Example6 = "2001:0db8:0001:0000:0000:0ab9:C0A8:0102";
hfzqasim: unfortunately, all above examples failed. I tried to send IR request with OpenSSL CMP client by providing this flag in the request -sans "2001:0db8:0001:0000:0000:0ab9:C0A8:0102" In the tcpdump, I was able to see that the request was having this IPv6 address, but the certificate I received does not have this IPv6 address. Please note that IPv4 is working fine. I am using EJBCA 7.11.0 Community
hfzqasim: Something interesting I found in this file https://github.com/Keyfactor/ejbca-ce/blob/bc595b13bf6d986b0a70337a41b4028ab7ae1276/modules/cesecore-common/src/org/cesecore/util/StringTools.java EJBCA is not converting ipOctets to string in the case of IPv6 address public static String ipOctetsToString(final byte[] octets) { String ret = null; if (octets.length == 4) { String ip = ""; // IPv4 address for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) { // What is going on there is that we are promoting a (signed) byte to int, // and then doing a bitwise AND operation on it to wipe out everything but // the first 8 bits. Because Java treats the byte as signed, if its unsigned // value is above > 127, the sign bit will be set, and it will appear to java // to be negative. When it gets promoted to int, bits 0 through 7 will be the // same as the byte, and bits 8 through 31 will be set to 1. So the bitwise // AND with 0x000000FF clears out all of those bits. final int intByte = 0x000000FF & octets[i]; final short t = (short) intByte; // NOPMD, we need short if (StringUtils.isNotEmpty(ip)) { ip += "."; } ip += t; } ret = ip; } // TODO: IPv6 return ret; }
primetomas: Yeah, that could be it for sure. I knew about this sometime ago but had forgotten about it. You wouldn't know how to convert the octet string into ipv6 readable text, and create a PR?
hfzqasim: Well, I am not the right person to make PR for such improvement in EJBCA-CE as I have never used Java. If it was Golang, I would have done that already.
primetomas: How would you do it in Go? Perhaps I can translate it to Java. I just don't have time to do that from scratch.
hfzqasim: Golang has builtin support for such conversions example code on Go playground https://go.dev/play/p/gwYbqsDEzyV
primetomas: Ok, java has basically the same. Try modifying the StringTools method to this. public static String ipOctetsToString(final byte[] octets) { String ret = null; if (octets.length == 4) { StringBuilder ip = new StringBuilder(StringUtils.EMPTY); // IPv4 address for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) { // What is going on there is that we are promoting a (signed) byte to int, // and then doing a bitwise AND operation on it to wipe out everything but // the first 8 bits. Because Java treats the byte as signed, if its unsigned // value is above > 127, the sign bit will be set, and it will appear to java // to be negative. When it gets promoted to int, bits 0 through 7 will be the // same as the byte, and bits 8 through 31 will be set to 1. So the bitwise // AND with 0x000000FF clears out all of those bits. final int intByte = 0x000000FF & octets[i]; final short t = (short) intByte; // NOPMD, we need short if (StringUtils.isNotEmpty(ip.toString())) { ip.append("."); } ip.append(t); } ret = ip.toString(); } else if (octets.length == 16) { try { ret = InetAddress.getByAddress(octets).getHostAddress(); } catch (UnknownHostException e) { e.printStackTrace(); if (log.isDebugEnabled()) { log.debug("Invalid IP address parsing octets: " + Hex.toHexString(octets)); } } } return ret; }
primetomas: The above code has been tested with CMP now. Will be released in the next major release. For reference: https://jira.primekey.se/browse/ECA-11440 (which is not available publicly yet, but will be in the future)

body_text:
When using SCEP with CA Mode, as per [EJBCA implementation of SCEP](https://araschnia.unam.mx/doc/SCEP.html), the CA that will be used to service the request is given by : _x000D_
_x000D_
> The CA used is either specified by the message or by the property scep.defaultca if no message is sent._x000D_
_x000D_
Another idea is that if the Scep Alias value is the same as the CA Name, then that could be used to select the CA. To achieve this, a small bit of code could be inserted into the GetCaName() menthod within org.ejbca.core.protocol.scep.ScepMessageDispatcherSessionBean as shown below:_x000D_
_x000D_
```_x000D_
     private String getCaName(final String caName, final ScepConfiguration scepConfiguration, final String alias) throws CADoesntExistsException {_x000D_
        if (scepConfiguration.getUseIntune(alias)) {_x000D_
            //Always return the scep_x000D_
            return scepConfiguration.getRADefaultCA(alias);_x000D_
        }_x000D_
_x000D_
        // _x000D_
        // Check if the 'alias' string indicates which CA to reference_x000D_
        // We do this by checking if a CA exists with same name as the alias._x000D_
        // Note: The CA name and alias must match exactly._x000D_
        log.info("Checking if a CA exists with the same name as the SCEP alias: "+alias);_x000D_
        CAInfo cainfo = caSession.getCAInfoInternal(-1, alias, true);_x000D_
        if (cainfo != null) {_x000D_
            return alias;_x000D_
        }_x000D_
        _x000D_
        if (!StringUtils.isEmpty(caName)) {_x000D_
            // Use the CA defined by the message if present_x000D_
            return caName;_x000D_
        }_x000D_
_x000D_
```

Why do you load 3 default policy in setupPolicyStore() function in unseal?_x000D_
it's a waste of time, if we use many default policies, do you get everyone from storage?_x000D_
_x000D_
// Ensure that the default policy exists, and if not, create it_x000D_
	if err := c.policyStore.loadACLPolicy(ctx, defaultPolicyName, defaultPolicy); err != nil {_x000D_
		return err_x000D_
	}_x000D_
	// Ensure that the response wrapping policy exists_x000D_
	if err := c.policyStore.loadACLPolicy(ctx, responseWrappingPolicyName, responseWrappingPolicy); err != nil {_x000D_
		return err_x000D_
	}_x000D_
	// Ensure that the control group policy exists_x000D_
	if err := c.policyStore.loadACLPolicy(ctx, controlGroupPolicyName, controlGroupPolicy); err != nil {_x000D_
		return err_x000D_
	}_x000D_

